\chapter{Statement loops and lexical exits}
\label{ch:17}
\index{Statement loops and lexical exits}

The intrinsic statement forms include \OnxCodeTerm{while}, \OnxCodeTerm{loop}, checked integer ranges, and a C-style \OnxCodeTerm{for}. A half-open range \OnxCodeTerm{a .. b} excludes the end, while \OnxCodeTerm{a ..= b} includes it. Labels resolve lexical exit targets. In a C-style loop, a continue to that loop executes its step. A continue to an outer loop must skip the rest of the inner body but still obey the outer loop's continuation rules.

\OnxInputCode[firstline=2,lastline=15]{../../on1x/examples/statement_loops.on1x}
The fixture also exercises an inclusive range at the maximum \OnxCodeTerm{i64}. A correct lowering must recognize the final element without forming the unrepresentable successor. Regression tests should cover an empty range, a one-element range, and a labeled transfer crossing an inner block with cleanup. Canonical lowering assigns resolved target identities so downstream checks can reject forged or out-of-scope exits. A value-producing exit has additional join obligations in \cref{ch:18}.

\section{Put the step on the right edge}
In the first loop in the listing the candidates are 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4.
Only the addition for 2 is skipped: the total after each candidate is
0, 1, 1, 4 and 8. Continuing at 2 advances the range. It neither ends
the range nor retries 2. A loop in which the user writes a step has
a similarly precise destination for each transfer.

For the second loop in the file, make one row for each value of the
outer index. At zero the inner body increases the step counter, and an
unlabeled break leaves the inner loop. Reaching the end of the outer
body runs its step. At one the counter increases again, but this time
the labeled continue jumps directly to the outer step. At two control
follows the first route again. Three increments of the counter and
three executions of the outer step have occurred when the condition
finally fails. Sending the continue to the outer condition without
running the step would keep testing the same index. Replacing it with
a labeled break would instead end the outer loop when the counter
was two. Those two erroneous destinations have quite different symptoms.

